<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apries</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 589–570 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Wahibre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apries was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the 26th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning from 589 to 570 BCE. He intervened in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and sent an army against the Greek colony of Cyrene, whose defeat at Irasa around 570 BCE provoked a mutiny. The general Amasis was proclaimed king in his place, and Apries was later killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apries.jpg" alt="Apries" width="199" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: de:Benutzer:Kl833x9; de:Benutzer:Daniel FR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apries.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Apries is the name by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and Diodorus designate Wahibre Haaibre, the fourth king of the Twenty-sixth dynasty counting from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a>. He inherited the throne from his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a>, in February 589 BCE. Manetho, who calls him Ouaphris, correctly records a reign of 19 years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-jeremiah">Book of Jeremiah</a> calls him Hophra. He was an active builder, adding to the temples at Athribis, Bahariya Oasis, Memphis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>, and in the fourth year of his reign his sister Ankhnesneferibre became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun">God's Wife of Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p><p>In 588 BCE he sent a force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to protect it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, but it withdrew quickly, apparently avoiding a major confrontation. Jerusalem fell after a siege of 18 months, in 587 or 586 BCE. His failed intervention was followed by a mutiny of the Aswan garrison. Classical historians say he campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, and a stela from Tahpanhes records that Nebuchadnezzar II tried to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 582 BCE and that Apries' forces repelled him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Around 570 BCE the Libyan king Adicran appealed to Apries against Greek settlers at Cyrene, who had been encouraged by Battus II. Apries sent an expedition and was decisively defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-irasa">Battle of Irasa</a>. On the army's return a civil war broke out between the native Egyptian troops and the foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a>, a general who had led a successful invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> in 592 BCE, and he declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> in 570 BCE. Apries fled abroad.</p><p>In 567 BCE Apries marched back with a Babylonian army and was probably killed in battle with Amasis' forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> instead holds that he was captured, treated well, and strangled after the Egyptian people demanded justice. Amasis is reported to have buried him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> with full military honours, and he married Apries' daughter Khedebneithirbinet II to legitimise his rule. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> that Apries erected at Sais was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and now stands in front of the basilica of Santa Maria sopra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apries” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Battus II brought new settlers and defeated an Egyptian army sent by <b>Apries</b> at Irasa around 570 BCE, and the reformer Demonax of Mantinea later limited royal power.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyrene">Kingdom of Cyrene</a></li></ul></section>
